-artificial-intelligence-bias">What is Artificial Intelligence Bias</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/who-is-the-father-of-artificial-intelligence">Who is the Father of Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/sop-for-ms-in-artificial-intelligence">SOP for MS in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/top-ai-penny-stocks-in-india">Top AI Penny Stocks in India</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-ai-in-data-analytics">What is AI in Data Analytics</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-business-management">Artificial Intelligence in Business Management</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/partial-order-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Partial Order Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-auditing">Artificial Intelligence in Auditing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-construction">Artificial Intelligence in Construction</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/information-retrieval-in-artificial-intelligence">Information Retrieval in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-psychology">Artificial Intelligence in Psychology</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/iterative-deepening-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Iterative Deepening Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/applications-of-deep-learning-in-artificial-intelligence">Applications of Deep Learning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/7-artificial-intelligence-projects-in-agriculture">7 Artificial Intelligence Projects in Agriculture</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-multimodal-ai">What is Multimodal AI</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/benefits-of-artificial-intelligence-in-business">Benefits of Artificial Intelligence in Business</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conceptual-graphs-in-artificial-intelligence">Conceptual Graphs in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/problem-space-and-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Problem Space and Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conditional-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Conditional Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/informed-search-algorithms-in-artificial-intelligence">Informed Search Algorithms in Artificial Intelligence</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading10"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse10" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse10" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading10" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse10"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="explainable-ai">next →</a> <a class="next" href="18-of-the-best-large-language-models-in-2024">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Artificial Intelligence in Ancient India - Texts and Vedas</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>14 Apr 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 11 min read</span></div><h2 class="h2">An Introduction to the Artificial Intelligence in Ancient India</h2><p>Ancient Indian texts and Vedas provide glimpses of profound wisdom that align with modern principles of Artificial Intelligence (AI). While AI as a concept wasn’t explicitly defined, scriptures like the Rigveda and Upanishads reflect early ideas of cognition, logic, and automation. The concept of "Chitta" (mind-stuff) and "Yantras" (mechanical devices) in these texts hint at a pursuit of understanding consciousness and creating tools to aid human tasks. Manuscripts like the Samhitas and Brahmanas describe the power of human intelligence to replicate natural processes, suggesting an intuitive grasp of mechanistic thinking. The stories of automated devices in the Mahabharata, like flying machines (Vimanas), and Ashoka's advanced machinery resonate with AI’s goals. These ancient insights underline India’s pioneering exploration of intelligence, blending philosophy and practical ingenuity.</p><h2 class="h2">Role of Books vis-a-vis Ancient Indian Texts</h2><p>Books have been vital in preserving and disseminating knowledge, serving as gateways to wisdom across ages. In the context of ancient Indian texts, their role transcends mere documentation. Texts like the Vedas, Upanishads, and epics such as the <a href="mahabharata-summary">Mahabharata</a> and Ramayana encapsulate profound philosophical, spiritual, and scientific insights, originally passed down orally before being inscribed. Unlike modern books focused on specific disciplines, these ancient texts adopt a holistic approach, weaving metaphysics, astronomy, medicine, and ethics into narratives and hymns. They emphasize the interconnectedness of knowledge, inspiring a quest for understanding life and the universe. Books act as bridges, allowing contemporary readers to engage with this timeless wisdom. Thus, the enduring legacy of ancient Indian texts highlights books as tools not just for learning but for connecting human consciousness with the eternal truths of existence.</p><p>Books have long played an important role in the preservation and transmission of knowledge, as well as in moulding human thought and society. Their importance in ancient Indian scriptures is extensive and complex. Ancient literature such as the Vedas, Upanishads, Mahabharata, and Ramayana were originally passed down orally, demonstrating a sophisticated system of memorization and transmission before being recorded on palm leaves or other mediums. These writings are more than just literary works; they are repositories of holistic knowledge that include spirituality, philosophy, science, medicine, and governance. They provide a window into the timeless wisdom of India, blending metaphysical ideas with practical teachings on life and society. Unlike modern books, which often focus on specialized topics, these texts adopt an integrative approach, addressing universal questions about existence, consciousness, and ethics. By preserving these ancient writings, books bridge the gap between past and present, enabling contemporary readers to access, interpret, and apply this wisdom in modern contexts.</p><p>Some gods, like Agni, who uses fire offerings to convey messages between humanity and the gods, and Indra, the thunder god, are frequently mentioned in the early Vedas. Some of these gods are diminished or replaced by other deities over time, while others persist in later Hinduism. Many modern Hindu practices are founded on the Vedas, which are revered as an eternal revelation and a source of unchanging knowledge.</p><h2 class="h2">Role of Artificial Intelligence</h2><p>The advent of <a href="what-is-big-data">big data</a>, <a href="introduction-to-cloud-computing">cloud computing</a>, <a href="artificial-neural-network">artificial neural networks</a>, and <a href="machine-learning">machine learning</a> has allowed engineers to develop computers capable of imitating human intellect. Building on these technologies, this research refers to machines that can detect, recognize, learn, react, and solve problems as <a href="artificial-intelligence-ai">artificial intelligence (AI)</a>.</p><p>Artificial intelligence enhances the speed, precision, and efficacy of human efforts. Financial organizations can use artificial intelligence algorithms to identify potentially fraudulent transactions, implement speedy and reliable credit scoring, and simplify labour-intensive data administration tasks.</p><p>Inevitably, smart technologies are set to revolutionize future workplaces. While AI can collaborate with humans and enhance their performance, it is emerging as a transformative innovation. Many view AI as a driving force of the fourth industrial revolution, with the potential to spark a revolution in education. Learning about AI has already started to feature in school curriculums, emphasizing its growing importance. However, much like television and computers, once hailed as game changers in education, AI may primarily improve access to information rather than significantly alter core educational practices. Despite this, AI offers unique opportunities to personalize learning, streamline administrative tasks, and provide adaptive teaching tools. It remains essential for educators to closely examine AI’s current capabilities, assess its limitations, and identify innovative pathways to enhance both teaching and learning experiences in an increasingly technology-driven world.</p><p>By using rule-based expert knowledge, the "first generation" of AI might assist human intellectual activity. The "second generation" might use a statistical or search model to discover the best answer, and the "third generation" will significantly enhance recognition performance using a brain model. Inevitably, smart technologies are set to revolutionize future workplaces. While AI can collaborate with humans and enhance their performance, it is emerging as a transformative innovation. Many view AI as a driving force of the fourth industrial revolution, with the potential to spark a revolution in education.</p><h3 class="h3">An Exploratory study on Origin of AI: Journey through the Ancient Indian Texts &amp; other technological descriptions, its past, present &amp; future</h3><p>This exploratory study delves into the origins of Artificial Intelligence (AI), tracing its conceptual roots through ancient Indian texts and technological descriptions. Ancient scriptures like the Vedas and epics such as the Mahabharata reveal insights into cognition, automation, and logic, laying a philosophical foundation for AI's evolution. By bridging ancient wisdom with modern advancements, the study examines AI’s journey from its speculative beginnings to its current transformative role across industries. Looking ahead, it explores AI’s potential to shape the future, offering a balanced perspective on its opportunities and challenges in redefining human interaction, innovation, and societal progress.</p><p>Geroge Devol created the first commercial, digital, and programmable robot in 1954. In the summer of 1956, a workshop on the Dartmouth College campus established the discipline of artificial intelligence research. However, the modern world is unaware that the origins of artificial intelligence (AI) date back between 8000 and 11000 years, when ancient Indian Rishis created myths, legends, and rumours about artificial beings possessing consciousness or intellect. Classical thinkers tried to characterize human thought as the mechanical manipulation of symbols, which sowed the seeds for current artificial intelligence. This work culminated in knowledge and ideas about many of the technical advancements we are seeing now that were previously expressed in Hinduism's sacred texts.</p><p>The Vedas, Upanishads, Mahabharata, and Bhagavad-Gita were thought to have been composed between 5000 and 8000 years ago (3000 BC to 6000 BC). These are not only the sacred epics of Hinduism, but they also serve as evidence of India's existence. From a modern scientific perspective, the writings of ancient Indian texts in Sanskrit, the oldest language in human history, are also considered to be in "natural language." The Upanishads, Advaita Siddhanta, and the nature of Maya all contain aspects of contemporary science that are comparable to contemporary scientific understanding. This awareness is further applied to the study of human mental processes and how to model them, which advances the field of artificial intelligence's natural language comprehension. Vedic ideas are both appropriate and essential for an effective leader.</p><p>In order to create a more intelligent and superior world with better machines, the idea is to bind man-made technology with the philosophy outlined in the Vedic texts. Natural language processing improves the human-machine interface by using natural language inputs and outputs. The notion of artificial intelligence is examined in this paper along with its historical roots in ancient Indian texts and other technological descriptions supported by contemporary scientific evidence. Sanskrit is also reviewed as a potential natural language input for computers.</p><h3 class="h3">Methodology &amp; Description of Artificial Intelligence in Ancient Indian Texts</h3><p>The methodology for exploring Artificial Intelligence (AI) in ancient Indian texts involves a detailed analysis of scriptures like the Vedas, Upanishads, and epics such as the Mahabharata and Ramayana. These texts are examined for references to cognitive processes, automated devices, and logical frameworks that align with AI concepts. Descriptions of "Yantras" (mechanical devices) and "Chitta" (mind-stuff) offer parallels to modern AI principles, highlighting early attempts to mimic human intelligence and functionality. By integrating textual analysis with comparative studies, this approach uncovers the philosophical and practical dimensions of AI in ancient India, showcasing its relevance to contemporary technological advancements.</p><p>The origins of artificial intelligence can be traced back to ancient mythology, when people dreamed of self-moving machines, automatons (or robots), artificial life, and other wonders long before technology made them possible. Around 2,700 years ago, around the reign of Homer, these oral tales concerning robots and other inventions were first recorded in writing. However, the idea of automatons and computers was not exclusive to the Greeks in antiquity. Similar stories can be found in the Ramayana, Mahabharata, and other epics. In Hindu mythology, the sorceress Maya and the engineer God Vishwakarma create automatons; in Greek mythology, the technological god Hephaestus and the skilled craftsman Daedalus create them. We believe that these myths are the first examples of science fiction stories ever written.</p><p>Using a form of qualitative approach, the current study examines artificial intelligence concealed in India's ancient texts. It focuses on secondary databases that contain ancient works including the Vedas, Ramayana, Mahabharata, Shrimad Bhagwat Geeta, Puranas, literatures, scriptures, and notes. A fresh perspective on the genesis of the idea of artificial intelligence and its existence and use in ancient Indian civilization has been attempted, based on historical research, literature, and documents.</p><p>The methodology for exploring Artificial Intelligence (AI) in ancient Indian texts involves an in-depth examination of scriptures like the Vedas, Upanishads, and epics such as the Mahabharata and Ramayana. These texts are analysed for references to cognitive processes, automated devices, and logical frameworks resonating with AI concepts. Descriptions of "Yantras" (mechanical devices), "Chitta" (mind-stuff), and even narratives of divine automation reveal early attempts to simulate intelligence and functionality.</p><h2 class="h2">Technological Descriptions with Modern Scientific Proofs</h2><p>The Vedas, Upanishads, and epics such as the Mahabharata contain intriguing references to technology that are congruent with modern scientific ideas. Both the Ramayana and the Sama Rangana Sutra Dhara give detailed descriptions of "Vimanas" (aerial vehicles), including flying devices, propulsion systems, and aerodynamic designs. Modern aeronautics makes remarkable parallels with these stories, showing a thorough understanding of mechanics and physics in ancient India. Similarly, "Yantras" (mechanical devices) discussed in works like as the Artha Shastra highlight advanced engineering techniques for automation and defence.</p><p>The concept of "Chitta" in the Yoga Sutras refers to the mind's processing power, which is consistent with modern AI and neural networks that simulate human cognition. Modern study has corroborated ancient astronomical estimates, such as those of Aryabhata about planetary motion and eclipses. Many of these descriptions are being reassessed for scientific validity through interdisciplinary research, such as archaeology and computational modelling. This combination of old knowledge and modern data demonstrates how timeless ideals influenced and spurred contemporary technical achievements, closing the gap among the past and the present.</p><p>Ancient Indian literature, such as the Vedas, Upanishads, and epics like the Mahabharata, contain fascinating references to technology that are consistent with modern scientific ideas.</p><p>The Ramayana and Sama Rangana Sutra Dhara describe "Vimanas" (aircraft) in detail, including flying devices, propulsion systems, and aerodynamic designs. Modern aeronautics finds significant connections to these narratives, implying an advanced understanding of mechanics and physics in ancient India. Similarly, "Yantras" (mechanical devices), referenced in works like the Artha Shastra, emphasize complex engineering concepts utilized for automation.</p><p>The idea of "Chitta" in the Yoga Sutras, a text which relates to the mind's processing power, is consistent with advanced artificial intelligence and neural networks that duplicate human intelligence. Modern study has verified the precision and profundity of ancient astronomical calculations, such as Aryabhata's on planetary motion and eclipses. The Charaka Samhita's mentions of diagnostics and surgical techniques show a scientific understanding of ancient medicine.</p><h3 class="h3">Present Effects of AI</h3><p>Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming multiple industries by increasing efficiency, accuracy, and decision-making processes. In the healthcare industry, artificial intelligence drives diagnostic instruments, personalized treatments, and robotic surgery. In financial services, artificial intelligence is used to detect fraud, automate trades, and assess risk. AI-powered automation is revolutionizing industries by simplifying supply chains and improving quality control. It enables more personalised e-commerce suggestions, smarter virtual assistants, and predictive marketing analytics. AI is also boosting education with adaptive learning systems, making it more accessible, and propelling progress in climate research and sustainability.</p><h3 class="h3">A Smarter Future</h3><p>The use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) is paving the way for a brighter future by transforming how we live and work. AI-powered enhancements have the potential to change healthcare through improved diagnostics, precision medicine, and automated healthcare systems. AI in education enables personalized learning experiences, whereas autonomous vehicles in transportation are reimagining mobility. Smart cities powered by artificial intelligence improve energy efficiency, public safety, and resource management. Furthermore, AI is transforming industries via automation, predictive analytics, and long-term solutions. This wiser future sees technology as a collaborator in enhancing human potential and creating a better world.</p><p>Smarter technology in our factories and places of employment, as well as networked gadgets capable of chatting, visualizing the entire supply chain, and making autonomous judgments, are just a few instances of how the Industrial Revolution may help firms succeed. One of the Fourth Industrial Revolution's most significant benefits is its ability to enhance the global population's standard of living and income. Our workplaces and enterprises become "smarter" and more "productive" as computers, humans, and linked devices collaborate to improve supply chains and warehouses.</p><p>Individuals can choose and develop personalized goods and services in a variety of businesses, including as travel, banking, savings, and insurance. Autonomous vehicles are now appearing in many cities throughout the world, with only the most difficult cases necessitating a human judge and complete court proceedings. Our intelligent assistants will now manage a wide range of duties in our lives, from travel planning to meeting preparation.</p><h2 class="h2">Imagining AI's Role in the Future</h2><p>Artificial intelligence (AI) is poised to alter all aspects of human life, ushering in a future in which technology augments human abilities. AI in healthcare will enable early diagnosis, precision medicine, and personalized treatments, potentially eliminating diseases and boosting life expectancy. AI-powered adaptive learning will improve education by providing tailored experiences for a wide range of learners and reducing educational disparities around the world. Autonomous vehicles, such as self-driving vehicles and drones, will transform mobility through making it safer and more efficient.</p><p>AI-powered smart cities will maximize resource usage, improve services for the public, and promote sustainability by increasing energy management and waste reduction. In industries, AI will increase automation, enhance decision-making using predictive analytics, and accelerate innovation in fields such as robotics and biotechnology. While AI has tremendous potential, ethical considerations and equal access must remain paramount.</p><h2 class="h2">Conclusion</h2><p>In Conclusion, Artificial Intelligence (AI) represents a significant step forward in human invention, connecting old wisdom with cutting-edge technology. Its origins in early conceptions of cognition and automation indicate humanity's enduring desire to reproduce and increase intelligence. Today, AI is transforming sectors, increasing efficiency, and addressing complex global concerns ranging from healthcare to education and sustainability. Looking ahead, its potential to alter daily life and change society systems is enormous. However, guaranteeing ethical development, equitable access, and responsible use is critical. By carefully incorporating AI, mankind can unleash its revolutionary power, fostering development and building a future that balances innovation and inclusivity.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="explainable-ai">Explainable AI</a></span></div><br/><br/><div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="18-of-the-best-large-language-models-in-2024" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="explainable-ai" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/><br/></td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="goals-of-artificial-intelligence"><h3 class="h3">Goals of Artificial Intelligence</h3><p class="related-post-content">AI can be achieved by reading the behavior of humans and using the results to develop intelligent systems. For example, they learn, make decisions and act in certain situations. Observing humans while problem-solving in simple tasks and using its results to develop intelligent systems. The overall research...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 7 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9